  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe threat for severe thunderstorms could return early next week\nas a strong upper high anchors over the four corner states and\nnorthwest flow becomes established from the northern Plains into\nthe northeast states. This fast northwest flow pattern favors\nkeeping decent amounts of deep layer vertical wind shear and\npotentially steep lapse rates at mid levels in play. There is a\ntypical amount of uncertainty/differences in location and \ntiming of short wave troughs topping the upper ridge and heading\nsoutheast across the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley area.\n\nLong-range guidance suggests one or more mesoscale convective\nsystems (MCSs) may develop over the Midwest before tracking into\nthe Ohio River Valley. Forecast soundings indicate an\nenvironment capable of supporting all severe weather hazards,\nincluding large hail, damaging wind gusts, tornadoes, and heavy\nrainfall. Machine learning guidance currently indicates a 5-30%\nprobability of severe thunderstorms from Sunday through Tuesday\nacross the Ohio River Valley. CSU Machine Learning has 5-10% \nchance of excessive rainfall Sunday and Monday and shifts things\nmostly south of area by Tuesday. The eventual evolution of this\nthreat will largely depend on the amplitude of the eastern U.S.\ntrough and the strength and position of the upper- level ridge,\nwhich will influence the track and intensity of any MCSs.",
